{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nbase='/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/mind'\nctx_path=f'{base}/outputs/space_overview/subspace--mind/context.json'\nwith open(ctx_path, encoding='utf-8') as f: ctx=json.load(f)\nprint('freshness', json.dumps(ctx.get('freshness',{}), ensure_ascii=False))\nprint('metadata', json.dumps(ctx.get('metadata',{}), ensure_ascii=False))\nsources={s['source_id']:s for s in ctx.get('source_records',[])}\nclaims_by_source=defaultdict(list)\nfor c in ctx.get('claims',[]):\n    claims_by_source[c.get('source_id')].append(c)\nfor sid, claims in claims_by_source.items():\n    s=sources.get(sid,{})\n    print('\\\\nSOURCE', sid)\n    print('TITLE', s.get('title'))\n    print('SUMMARY', s.get('summary'))\n    for c in claims:\n        print('-', c['claim_id'])\n        print('  short:', c.get('short_title'))\n        print('  text:', c.get('text'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nbase='/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/mind'\nctx_path=f'{base}/outputs/space_overview/subspace--mind/context.json'\nwith open(ctx_path, encoding='utf-8') as f: ctx=json.load(f)\nprint('freshness', json.dumps(ctx.get('freshness',{}), ensure_ascii=False))\nprint('metadata', json.dumps(ctx.get('metadata',{}), ensure_ascii=False))\nsources={s['source_id']:s for s in ctx.get('source_records',[])}\nclaims_by_source=defaultdict(list)\nfor c in ctx.get('claims',[]):\n    claims_by_source[c.get('source_id')].append(c)\nfor sid, claims in claims_by_source.items():\n    s=sources.get(sid,{})\n    print('\\\\nSOURCE', sid)\n    print('TITLE', s.get('title'))\n    print('SUMMARY', s.get('summary'))\n    for c in claims:\n        print('-', c['claim_id'])\n        print('  short:', c.get('short_title'))\n        print('  text:', c.get('text'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"freshness {\"claim_count\": 66, \"generated_at\": \"2026-04-26T18:06:35Z\", \"input_signature\": \"sha256:041d0c015c250632a95831f2318efb7c675050d23770c785255f74791447e22f\", \"relation_count\": 16, \"source_record_count\": 5, \"topic_count\": 10}\nmetadata {\"overview_id\": \"subspace--mind\", \"parent_space_name\": \"philosophy\", \"scope_kind\": \"subspace\", \"scope_name\": \"mind\", \"scope_title\": \"Mind\", \"space_name\": \"mind\"}\n\nSOURCE source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78\nTITLE 02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention--w2132002500\nSUMMARY Jha, Krompinger, and Baime report that mindfulness training changes ANT-measured attention subsystems in different ways depending on training history. Novice MBSR participants showed improved orienting after an 8-week course, experienced retreat participants showed altered alerting and faster no-cue detection after a 1-month retreat, and experienced meditators already showed reduced conflict interference at baseline. The findings are behavioral and preliminary, with important limits from group size, prior-experience differences, task repetition, and lack of active-training controls.\n- claim-a-1-month-intensive-mindfulness-retreat-among-previously-experienced-meditators--cd2a814c579a\n  short: Retreat improves alerting\n  text: A 1-month intensive mindfulness retreat among previously experienced meditators improves exogenous alerting-related detection when no target cue is provided.\n- claim-an-8-week-mindfulness-based-stress-reduction-course-improves-endogenous-orientin--f7bde69b7431\n  short: MBSR improves orienting\n  text: An 8-week mindfulness-based stress reduction course improves endogenous orienting relative to control and retreat groups at post-training testing.\n- claim-mindfulness-training-can-improve-attention-related-behavior-by-changing-specific--f9e8e6ee54d7\n  short: Training modifies attention subsystems\n  text: Mindfulness training can improve attention-related behavior by changing specific attentional subsystems rather than producing a single global attention benefit.\n- claim-participants-with-prior-concentrative-meditation-experience-show-reduced-conflic--7b44607a9505\n  short: Experience reduces conflict\n  text: Participants with prior concentrative meditation experience show reduced conflict-monitoring interference at baseline relative to meditation-naive participants.\n- claim-post-training-conflict-monitoring-differences-are-not-detected-among-the-mbsr-re--62de62c3ee08\n  short: Conflict gains fade\n  text: Post-training conflict-monitoring differences are not detected among the MBSR, retreat, and control groups, possibly because task repetition improves conflict scores in controls.\n- claim-the-attention-network-test-separates-alerting-orienting-and-conflict-monitoring--08b2302acb19\n  short: ANT partitions attention\n  text: The Attention Network Test separates alerting, orienting, and conflict monitoring by subtracting performance across predefined cue and target conditions.\n- claim-the-attentional-effects-should-be-treated-as-preliminary-because-the-study-used--8a181621f4d7\n  short: Findings require caution\n  text: The attentional effects should be treated as preliminary because the study used small groups, no active-training control, behavioral measures only, and imperfect control over training protocols.\n- claim-within-the-retreat-group-greater-prior-meditation-experience-predicts-a-smaller--3ca73021a60e\n  short: Experience predicts alerting\n  text: Within the retreat group, greater prior meditation experience predicts a smaller Time 2 alerting score, consistent with a more readied attentional state.\n\nSOURCE source-01-an-information-integration-theory-of-consciousness-w2114900706--09db8f102f39\nTITLE 01-an-information-integration-theory-of-consciousness--w2114900706\nSUMMARY Tononi’s 2004 article argues that consciousness is identical with a system’s capacity to integrate information. The theory defines Φ as effective information across a complex’s minimum information bipartition, uses complexes to delimit subjects of experience, and uses effective-information relationships to explain the quality of experience. It applies the framework to thalamocortical organization, modular cerebellar-like systems, afferent/efferent pathways, arousal systems, split-brain cases, sleep, seizures, and the possibility of graded consciousness in animals, infants, and artifacts.\n- claim-a-complex-is-an-integrated-subset-with-greater-than-zero-that-is-not-contained-i--70401a6fb458\n  short: Complexes are experiential subjects\n  text: A complex is an integrated subset with Φ greater than zero that is not contained in a larger subset with higher Φ, and complexes are the loci of subjective experience.\n- claim-a-distributed-thalamocortical-network-is-well-suited-to-generate-consciousness-b--571db463385d\n  short: Thalamocortical networks maximize Phi\n  text: A distributed thalamocortical network is well suited to generate consciousness because it combines functional specialization with functional integration.\n- claim-anatomical-or-functional-disconnection-within-thalamocortical-systems-can-reduce--89adceb072c5\n  short: Disconnection reduces conscious level\n  text: Anatomical or functional disconnection within thalamocortical systems can reduce, split, or selectively alter consciousness by changing the system’s integrated-information structure.\n- claim-consciousness-corresponds-to-a-system-s-capacity-to-integrate-information-becaus--ca44bbf79dcb\n  short: Consciousness integrates information\n  text: Consciousness corresponds to a system’s capacity to integrate information, because conscious experience is both highly differentiated and unified.\n- claim-consciousness-is-graded-rather-than-all-or-none-and-could-in-principle-exist-in--76ebdb9cb6bc\n  short: High Phi enables artifacts\n  text: Consciousness is graded rather than all-or-none and could in principle exist in infants, animals, or artificial systems when they possess complexes with sufficiently high Φ.\n- claim-each-particular-conscious-experience-is-specified-by-the-current-activity-values--ef679659733b\n  short: Current states specify experiences\n  text: Each particular conscious experience is specified by the current activity values of the variables that mediate informational interactions within a complex.\n- claim-reticular-sensory-afferent-motor-efferent-and-subcortical-loop-activity-can-modu--173db43834c9\n  short: Ports influence without belonging\n  text: Reticular, sensory afferent, motor efferent, and subcortical loop activity can modulate or affect the thalamocortical main complex without directly contributing to conscious experience.\n- claim-strongly-modular-neural-architectures-can-be-computationally-sophisticated-yet-c--212c143093c3\n  short: Modularity lowers conscious integration\n  text: Strongly modular neural architectures can be computationally sophisticated yet contribute little to consciousness because they generate many small low-Φ complexes rather than one high-Φ complex.\n- claim-the-quality-of-consciousness-is-specified-by-the-informational-relationships-amo--52a77799b51d\n  short: Qualia space determines quality\n  text: The quality of consciousness is specified by the informational relationships among the elements of a complex, represented as an effective-information-defined qualia space.\n- claim-the-quantity-of-consciousness-available-to-a-system-is-measured-by-the-value-of--48abc06d417d\n  short: Phi measures conscious quantity\n  text: The quantity of consciousness available to a system is measured by the Φ value of a complex, where Φ is effective information across the minimum information bipartition.\n\nSOURCE source-04-mind-the-hype-a-critical-evaluation-and-prescriptive-agenda-for-research-on-mind-w2761197918--79c0d54db997\nTITLE 04-mind-the-hype-a-critical-evaluation-and-prescriptive-agenda-for-research-on-mind--w2761197918\nSUMMARY Van Dam and colleagues argue that mindfulness research has been promoted faster than its evidence base can support. The source identifies semantic ambiguity, weak measurement, immature clinical trials, underreported adverse effects, and overinterpreted neuroimaging as major reasons for a more cautious and rigorous research agenda.\n- claim-adverse-effects-are-undermeasured--25fa20260426\n  short: Adverse effects are undermeasured\n  text: Meditation-related adverse effects and opportunity costs are plausible, documented, and undermeasured, so safety claims based on passive reporting or absent evidence are unwarranted.\n- claim-adverse-event-assessment-in-meditation-research-needs-active-monitoring-rigorous--ae38800857f2\n  short: Adverse events need monitoring\n  text: Adverse-event assessment in meditation research needs active monitoring, rigorous trial designs, and clinical interviews rather than passive safety assumptions.\n- claim-clinical-evidence-remains-preliminary--117717456958\n  short: Clinical evidence remains preliminary\n  text: Clinical evidence for mindfulness-based interventions remains preliminary because much intervention research has not advanced beyond early-stage efficacy designs and often lacks active controls or real-world practice tests.\n- claim-common-mindfulness-questionnaires-operationalize-different-facets-of-mindfulness--19920139d48a\n  short: Measures capture different facets\n  text: Common mindfulness questionnaires operationalize different facets of mindfulness, so comparisons across studies require scrutiny of exactly which construct each measure captures.\n- claim-contemplative-neuroscience-findings-about-mindfulness-are-vulnerable-to-oversimp--944ad1b9ad23\n  short: Neuroimaging evidence is limited\n  text: Contemplative neuroscience findings about mindfulness are vulnerable to oversimplified public interpretation, group-level averaging, cross-sectional confounds, movement and cardiorespiratory artifacts, and uncertain practical significance.\n- claim-existing-mindfulness-self-report-measures-have-unresolved-construct-validity-lim--65891880856d\n  short: Self reports remain uncertain\n  text: Existing mindfulness self-report measures have unresolved construct-validity limits and may not track exactly what practice teaches.\n- claim-future-mindfulness-research-should-replace-generic-labels-with-precise-descripti--0291ee9277c4\n  short: Specific protocols improve research\n  text: Future mindfulness research should replace generic labels with precise descriptions of meditation features, intervention protocols, teacher characteristics, participant histories, controls, and adverse-event monitoring.\n- claim-future-mindfulness-science-should-emphasize-preregistration-open-science-replica--ad9bbbab2603\n  short: Future science needs rigor\n  text: Future mindfulness science should emphasize preregistration, open-science replications, stronger clinical controls, adverse-event warnings, and modest communication of neuroimaging results.\n- claim-meditation-can-be-associated-with-clinically-significant-adverse-experiences-and--ee33e7577df8\n  short: Meditation can harm\n  text: Meditation can be associated with clinically significant adverse experiences, and assumptions of minimal adverse effects rest more on lack of research than substantive evidence.\n- claim-meditation-related-adverse-effects-are-under-assessed-in-current-trials-so-claim--ce66cec14da3\n  short: Adverse effects are underassessed\n  text: Meditation-related adverse effects are under-assessed in current trials, so claims that meditation is free of adverse effects rest on inadequate evidence.\n- claim-mindfulness-and-meditation-practices-can-be-contraindicated-for-some-psychiatric--6fe966926b20\n  short: Meditation has contraindications\n  text: Mindfulness and meditation practices can be contraindicated for some psychiatric conditions and vulnerable populations without appropriate clinical monitoring.\n- claim-mindfulness-based-interventions-show-a-mixed-pattern-of-moderate-low-or-absent-e--2eba873d6241\n  short: Efficacy is mixed\n  text: Mindfulness-based interventions show a mixed pattern of moderate, low, or absent efficacy depending on outcome and disorder.\n- claim-mindfulness-based-interventions-show-mixed-and-condition-dependent-clinical-effi--ada76b44a400\n  short: MBI efficacy is mixed\n  text: Mindfulness-based interventions show mixed and condition-dependent clinical efficacy rather than uniformly strong therapeutic benefit.\n- claim-mindfulness-based-interventions-vary-substantially-in-content-delivery-instructo--8ecee4c072be\n  short: Interventions vary substantially\n  text: Mindfulness-based interventions vary substantially in content, delivery, instructor training, homework, and participant population, limiting broad efficacy claims across interventions.\n- claim-mindfulness-definition-is-ambiguous--4f220365d6f4\n  short: Mindfulness is ambiguous\n  text: The term mindfulness lacks a universally accepted technical definition and often conflates heterogeneous mental faculties, practices, interventions, and measurement targets.\n- claim-mindfulness-hype-can-create-opportunity-costs-by-diverting-people-from-establish--1a47df8a5e6d\n  short: Hype imposes opportunity costs\n  text: Mindfulness hype can create opportunity costs by diverting people from established treatments or other activities more likely to help their condition.\n- claim-mindfulness-hype-exceeds-evidence--96cb023af909\n  short: Hype exceeds evidence\n  text: Mindfulness and meditation have become widely promoted in clinical, educational, corporate, military, and media contexts despite an evidence base that remains uneven and vulnerable to exaggeration.\n- claim-mindfulness-is-not-a-unitary-construct-it-is-an-umbrella-term-spanning-diverse-p--ab25afeeb846\n  short: Mindfulness is semantically broad\n  text: Mindfulness is not a unitary construct; it is an umbrella term spanning diverse practices, processes, and characteristics related to attention, awareness, memory, acceptance, and discernment.\n- claim-mindfulness-lacks-a-universally-accepted-technical-definition-so-empirical-findi--b873cbc87071\n  short: Mindfulness lacks shared definition\n  text: Mindfulness lacks a universally accepted technical definition, so empirical findings require careful specification of the mental states, practices, and interventions under study.\n- claim-mindfulness-research-should-adopt-preregistration-open-science-replication-caref--5f747418bb67\n  short: Rigor should increase\n  text: Mindfulness research should adopt preregistration, open-science replication, careful terminology, stronger controls, systematic adverse-event assessment, and modest reporting of neuroimaging results.\n- claim-neuroimaging-findings-about-mindfulness-are-preliminary-and-vulnerable-to-oversi--d3431c27c5c9\n  short: Brain findings stay preliminary\n  text: Neuroimaging findings about mindfulness are preliminary and vulnerable to oversimplification, group-level confounding, physical artifacts, and unclear practical significance.\n- claim-neuroimaging-requires-caution--201770958900\n  short: Neuroimaging requires caution\n  text: Neuroimaging evidence on meditation can suggest modest brain differences, but group averages, cross-sectional designs, cardiorespiratory artifacts, head motion, and interpretive oversimplification limit causal and practical conclusions.\n- claim-research-needs-prescriptive-reform--79c0d54db997\n  short: Research needs reform\n  text: Future mindfulness research should use precise terminology, stronger construct measures, active controls, preregistration, adverse-event monitoring, detailed intervention reporting, and cautious public communication.\n- claim-research-on-mindfulness-should-shift-from-seeking-one-unitary-mindfulness-measur--210f23dc10a0\n  short: Specific functions should replace unity\n  text: Research on mindfulness should shift from seeking one unitary mindfulness measure toward measuring specific biological, emotional, cognitive, behavioral, and social functions changed by training.\n- claim-self-report-measures-lack-validity--56550542660a\n  short: Self reports lack validity\n  text: Self-report mindfulness questionnaires often have weak construct validity because scale meanings, factor structures, item interpretation, social desirability, and introspective limitations vary across participants and practice histories.\n- claim-the-clinical-evidence-base-for-mindfulness-based-interventions-is-too-immature-t--464dc35b525d\n  short: Clinical evidence remains immature\n  text: The clinical evidence base for mindfulness-based interventions is too immature to justify broad real-world implementation without stronger controls and later-stage research.\n- claim-the-clinical-evidence-base-for-mindfulness-based-interventions-remains-insuffici--fd5df4ed74a7\n  short: Clinical evidence remains immature\n  text: The clinical evidence base for mindfulness-based interventions remains insufficiently mature for broad routine clinical implementation.\n- claim-therapeutic-benefits-are-condition-specific--201401200700\n  short: Benefits are condition specific\n  text: Mindfulness-based therapies may help some people and conditions, particularly some depression-related contexts, but they should not be treated as broadly superior to established treatments.\n- claim-undifferentiated-use-of-mindfulness-and-meditation-can-make-brief-app-exercises--0796f143a40c\n  short: Umbrella labels distort comparison\n  text: Undifferentiated use of mindfulness and meditation can make brief app exercises, intensive retreats, questionnaires, and long-term practice appear falsely comparable.\n\nSOURCE source-03-the-role-of-alpha-band-brain-oscillations-as-a-sensory-suppression-mechanism-dur-w2005105933--7b6b7d5bc8b9\nTITLE 03-the-role-of-alpha-band-brain-oscillations-as-a-sensory-suppression-mechanism-dur--w2005105933\nSUMMARY Foxe and Snyder review evidence that alpha-band oscillations, especially 8-14 Hz activity, function as an active suppressive mechanism during selective attention. Across intersensory, spatial, feature-based, auditory, and tactile attention tasks, alpha power tends to increase in cortical regions responsible for processing irrelevant or distracting information, and causal and predictive evidence links stronger alpha to reduced visual excitability and weaker perception.\n- claim-alpha-band-oscillations-can-be-actively-invoked-in-cortical-regions-that-process--09466b39d396\n  short: Alpha suppresses distractors\n  text: Alpha-band oscillations can be actively invoked in cortical regions that process irrelevant or distracting information, making alpha a mechanism of attentional suppression.\n- claim-alpha-mediated-biasing-extends-beyond-vision-appearing-in-auditory-and-tactile-s--2cd546facc83\n  short: Alpha bias spans senses\n  text: Alpha-mediated biasing extends beyond vision, appearing in auditory and tactile spatial attention with increases over cortical regions ipsilateral to the cued direction.\n- claim-attention-related-alpha-modulation-appears-primarily-controlled-by-cortico-corti--1eb91088cf2b\n  short: Cortical networks control alpha\n  text: Attention-related alpha modulation appears primarily controlled by cortico-cortical frontal and parietal networks, with thalamo-cortical connections possibly contributing secondarily.\n- claim-during-visual-spatial-attention-alpha-band-power-increases-over-occipital-cortex--09a99b87f400\n  short: Spatial alpha targets distractors\n  text: During visual spatial attention, alpha-band power increases over occipital cortex ipsilateral to the attended side and retinotopically targets areas likely to receive distracters.\n- claim-feature-based-visual-attention-increases-alpha-power-in-dorsal-or-ventral-stream--0c7646ad3b93\n  short: Feature alpha suppresses irrelevant streams\n  text: Feature-based visual attention increases alpha power in dorsal or ventral stream regions when those regions process an irrelevant feature.\n- claim-higher-naturally-occurring-or-experimentally-induced-alpha-power-reduces-visual--3884cfa09119\n  short: Alpha lowers visual excitability\n  text: Higher naturally occurring or experimentally induced alpha power reduces visual cortical excitability and predicts weaker perceptual detection.\n- claim-in-intersensory-attention-tasks-attending-to-audition-increases-8-14-hz-parieto--6ff6ff285662\n  short: Auditory attention gates vision\n  text: In intersensory attention tasks, attending to audition increases 8-14 Hz parieto-occipital alpha activity before the audiovisual target, consistent with anticipatory gating of visual processing.\n- claim-instructional-cuing-paradigms-distinguish-distracter-suppression-from-probabilis--af0b5b81b7e7\n  short: Instructional cues isolate distractors\n  text: Instructional cuing paradigms distinguish distracter suppression from probabilistic orienting because uncued locations never require a response.\n- claim-sustained-focal-alpha-increases-above-pre-cue-baseline-during-rapid-bilateral-st--8699d621dccc\n  short: Alpha is active suppression\n  text: Sustained focal alpha increases above pre-cue baseline during rapid bilateral stimulation support an active suppression account rather than a mere return to cortical idling.\n\nSOURCE source-05-self-awareness-self-regulation-and-self-transcendence-s-art-a-framework-for-unde-w2005022581--615ee4e1923f\nTITLE 05-self-awareness-self-regulation-and-self-transcendence-s-art-a-framework-for-unde--w2005022581\nSUMMARY Vago and Silbersweig propose S-ART as a neurobiological framework for mindfulness, organizing practice-related change around self-awareness, self-regulation, and self-transcendence. The review argues that mindfulness practices can reduce biased narrative self-processing through interdependent mechanisms including attention and emotion regulation, extinction and reconsolidation, prosociality, non-attachment, and decentering, while calling for more precise longitudinal research on dose, practice quality, component mechanisms, and adverse effects.\n- claim-biased-narrative-self-processing-can-reify-dysfunctional-self-schemas-through-af--544c2f60e4c0\n  short: Narrative bias reifies schemas\n  text: Biased narrative self-processing can reify dysfunctional self-schemas through affect-biased attention, rumination, and repeated maladaptive interpretation.\n- claim-future-mindfulness-research-needs-longitudinal-measurement-of-s-art-subcomponent--a2496826340c\n  short: Research needs longitudinal rigor\n  text: Future mindfulness research needs longitudinal measurement of S-ART subcomponents, dose effects, practice quality, and possible adverse effects.\n- claim-intention-motivation-attention-regulation-emotion-regulation-extinction-and-reco--002095abdbf2\n  short: Multiple mechanisms support S-ART\n  text: Intention, motivation, attention regulation, emotion regulation, extinction and reconsolidation, prosociality, non-attachment, and decentering are proposed as interdependent mechanisms supporting S-ART.\n- claim-meditation-practice-is-associated-with-physiological-profiles-of-relaxed-alertne--a46fa752f290\n  short: Meditation shifts physiology\n  text: Meditation practice is associated with physiological profiles of relaxed alertness, including reduced sympathetic markers and increased parasympathetic activity.\n- claim-mindfulness-based-meditation-is-proposed-to-modulate-self-specifying-and-narrati--0ff3acc89706\n  short: Fronto-parietal control integrates self\n  text: Mindfulness-based meditation is proposed to modulate self-specifying and narrative self networks through an integrative fronto-parietal control network.\n- claim-mindfulness-based-stress-reduction-and-compassion-meditation-have-been-reported--d470d02dbd8f\n  short: Practice alters stress markers\n  text: Mindfulness-based stress reduction and compassion meditation have been reported to influence immune and stress-reactivity markers.\n- claim-mindfulness-can-be-modeled-as-systematic-mental-training-that-reduces-biased-sel--6f2cabe951a3\n  short: Mindfulness reduces self-bias\n  text: Mindfulness can be modeled as systematic mental training that reduces biased self-processing by developing self-awareness, self-regulation, and self-transcendence.\n- claim-s-art-distinguishes-experiential-enactive-experiential-phenomenological-and-narr--3e3013b46122\n  short: Self-processing has three systems\n  text: S-ART distinguishes experiential enactive, experiential phenomenological, and narrative self-processing systems as targets of mindfulness-related modulation.\n- claim-s-art-treats-mindfulness-as-a-multidimensional-skillset-rather-than-a-single-uni--019ffda0022f\n  short: Mindfulness is multidimensional\n  text: S-ART treats mindfulness as a multidimensional skillset rather than a single unitary cognitive capacity.\n- claim-short-mindfulness-interventions-have-been-reported-to-improve-clinical-symptoms--38a77abb1074\n  short: Brief training can change outcomes\n  text: Short mindfulness interventions have been reported to improve clinical symptoms, executive function, and white matter connectivity, although dosage and quality questions remain unresolved.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
